### What is Flink's Async I/O?

Without Async I/O, a normal map or process function that calls an external system blocks the operator thread for the full round-trip. If each lookup takes twenty milliseconds, a single task can do at most fifty lookups per second. Async I/O decouples request issuance from response handling so many requests are outstanding at once.

```java
package com.bank.enrichment;

import org.apache.flink.streaming.api.datastream.AsyncDataStream;
import org.apache.flink.streaming.api.datastream.DataStream;
import org.apache.flink.streaming.api.environment.StreamExecutionEnvironment;
import java.util.concurrent.TimeUnit;

public class AsyncIoDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        StreamExecutionEnvironment env =
            StreamExecutionEnvironment.getExecutionEnvironment();

        DataStream<Transaction> transactions =
            env.addSource(new KafkaSource<>("nbi-transactions"));

        // Async I/O: enrich with credit bureau risk score
        DataStream<EnrichedTransaction> enriched =
            AsyncDataStream.unorderedWait(
                transactions,
                new CreditBureauAsyncFunction(),
                30, TimeUnit.SECONDS,  // timeout
                100);                   // capacity: max 100 in-flight

        enriched.addSink(new FraudScorerSink());
        env.execute("NBI Async I/O Enrichment");
    }
}
```

### How do you implement an AsyncFunction?

You implement `AsyncFunction` whose `asyncInvoke` method receives an input record and a `ResultFuture`. Inside it you kick off the external call using a non-blocking client and complete the ResultFuture with the results when the response arrives.

```java
package com.bank.enrichment;

import org.apache.flink.streaming.api.functions.async.AsyncFunction;
import org.apache.flink.streaming.api.functions.async.ResultFuture;
import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.Collections;
import java.util.concurrent.CompletableFuture;

public class CreditBureauAsyncFunction
        implements AsyncFunction<Transaction, EnrichedTransaction> {

    private transient AsyncCreditBureauClient client;

    @Override
    public void open(Configuration parameters) {
        // Initialize async HTTP client (non-blocking)
        client = new AsyncCreditBureauClient("https://bureau.nbi.in");
    }

    @Override
    public void asyncInvoke(Transaction txn,
            ResultFuture<EnrichedTransaction> resultFuture) {
        // Fire non-blocking request
        CompletableFuture<Double> scoreFuture =
            client.getRiskScore(txn.getAccountId());

        // Complete ResultFuture when response arrives
        scoreFuture.thenAccept(score -> {
            EnrichedTransaction enriched = new EnrichedTransaction(
                txn.getAccountId(),
                txn.getAmount(),
                txn.getEventTime(),
                BigDecimal.valueOf(score));
            resultFuture.complete(
                Collections.singleton(enriched));
        }).exceptionally(ex -> {
            resultFuture.complete(
                Collections.singleton(
                    txn.withDefaultScore())); // fallback
            return null;
        });
    }

    @Override
    public void close() {
        client.close();
    }
}
```

### What is the difference between unorderedWait and orderedWait?

| Feature | unorderedWait | orderedWait |
|---|---|---|
| Result order | Completion order (fastest first) | Original input order preserved |
| Throughput | Maximum (no head-of-line blocking) | Slightly lower (buffers early results) |
| Watermark handling | Respects watermark boundaries | Preserves strict order |
| Memory usage | Lower | Higher (buffers pending results) |
| Use when | Order does not matter downstream | Downstream depends on record order |

```java
// unorderedWait: maximum throughput, results may arrive out of order
DataStream<EnrichedTransaction> unordered =
    AsyncDataStream.unorderedWait(
        transactions,
        new CreditBureauAsyncFunction(),
        30, TimeUnit.SECONDS, 100);

// orderedWait: preserves input order, buffers early responses
DataStream<EnrichedTransaction> ordered =
    AsyncDataStream.orderedWait(
        transactions,
        new CreditBureauAsyncFunction(),
        30, TimeUnit.SECONDS, 100);
```

### How do capacity and timeout govern behavior?

Capacity caps the number of in-flight requests. When the limit is reached, the operator blocks new requests, creating natural backpressure. Timeout defines how long to wait for a response before calling the timeout handler.

```java
package com.bank.enrichment;

import org.apache.flink.streaming.api.functions.async.AsyncFunction;
import org.apache.flink.streaming.api.functions.async.ResultFuture;
import java.util.Collections;
import java.util.concurrent.TimeoutException;

public class TimeoutHandlingDemo
        implements AsyncFunction<Transaction, EnrichedTransaction> {

    @Override
    public void asyncInvoke(Transaction txn,
            ResultFuture<EnrichedTransaction> resultFuture) {
        client.getRiskScore(txn.getAccountId())
            .thenAccept(score -> {
                resultFuture.complete(Collections.singleton(
                    txn.withScore(score)));
            });
    }

    @Override
    public void timeout(Transaction txn,
            ResultFuture<EnrichedTransaction> resultFuture) {
        // Called when request exceeds timeout
        // Option 1: complete with fallback value
        resultFuture.complete(Collections.singleton(
            txn.withDefaultScore())); // never lose the record

        // Option 2: fail the job (strict mode)
        // resultFuture.completeExceptionally(
        //     new TimeoutException("Bureau timeout for "
        //         + txn.getAccountId()));
    }
}
```

```text
Capacity and timeout behavior:

  Capacity = 100, Timeout = 30s

  Requests in flight: 98, 99, 100
  --> new records WAIT (backpressure) until a slot opens

  Request for ACC-001 sent at t=0
  No response by t=30s
  --> timeout() called
  --> provide fallback score (no data loss)
```

### What is the common mistake with Async I/O?

The actual asynchrony must come from a genuinely non-blocking client. Wrapping a blocking JDBC call in a thread pool inside asyncInvoke is a common mistake that does not give real concurrency and can exhaust threads.

```java
// WRONG: wrapping blocking call in thread pool
@Override
public void asyncInvoke(Transaction txn,
        ResultFuture<EnrichedTransaction> resultFuture) {
    // This blocks a thread from the pool for each call
    // With 100 capacity, you need 100 threads!
    CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(() -> {
        // BLOCKING JDBC call
        return jdbcClient.querySync(txn.getAccountId());
    }, threadPool).thenAccept(result -> {
        resultFuture.complete(Collections.singleton(result));
    });
}

// CORRECT: use a genuinely async client
@Override
public void asyncInvoke(Transaction txn,
        ResultFuture<EnrichedTransaction> resultFuture) {
    // Non-blocking: uses NIO under the hood
    asyncHttpClient.getRiskScore(txn.getAccountId())
        .thenAccept(score -> {
            resultFuture.complete(Collections.singleton(
                txn.withScore(score)));
        });
}
```

### How does Async I/O integrate with checkpointing?

In-flight requests are part of the operator's state. When a checkpoint occurs, the pending requests are stored in the snapshot. On recovery, Flink retries them correctly, preserving exactly-once semantics.

```text
Checkpointing with Async I/O:

  In-flight requests: [ACC-001, ACC-002, ACC-003]
  Checkpoint triggered:
    - Snapshot includes: pending requests + their input records
  Checkpoint confirmed.

  Crash and recovery:
    - Restore from checkpoint
    - In-flight requests [ACC-001, ACC-002, ACC-003] retried
    - Each record processed exactly once in the output
```

### What about the async lookup join in Flink SQL?

Flink SQL exposes the same capability declaratively through the async lookup join. You get Async I/O benefits without writing the AsyncFunction by hand.

```sql
-- Async lookup join in Flink SQL
SELECT t.account_id, t.amount, r.risk_score
FROM nbi_transactions AS t
JOIN credit_bureau FOR SYSTEM_TIME AS OF t.txn_time AS r
  ON t.account_id = r.account_id;

-- The lookup table 'credit_bureau' is backed by an async connector
-- Flink automatically issues non-blocking lookups
-- No AsyncFunction code needed
```

```java
// Equivalent SQL table definition:
// CREATE TABLE credit_bureau (
//   account_id STRING,
//   risk_score DOUBLE,
//   PRIMARY KEY (account_id) NOT ENFORCED
// ) WITH (
//   'connector' = 'jdbc',
//   'lookup.async' = 'true',
//   'lookup.max-retries' = '3'
// );
```

### What should a senior engineer know about Async I/O?

A senior engineer should set capacity to match the external system's concurrency limits, implement the timeout callback to provide a fallback rather than silently drop records, use unorderedWait as the default for throughput, and know that the async lookup join in Flink SQL provides the same capability declaratively.

```java
package com.bank.enrichment;

public class SeniorAsyncIoChecklist {
    // Senior engineer's Async I/O checklist:
    //
    // 1. Client MUST be genuinely non-blocking (NIO-based)
    // 2. Capacity = external system's concurrency limit
    //    (too high overwhelms backend, too low underutilizes it)
    // 3. Timeout > expected p99 latency of external system
    // 4. Timeout handler: provide fallback, never silently drop
    // 5. Default to unorderedWait for throughput
    // 6. Use orderedWait only when downstream needs strict order
    // 7. Monitor: in-flight count, timeout rate, latency p99
    // 8. Consider Flink SQL async lookup join for simpler cases
}
```
